---
import CaseStudy from '../layouts/CaseStudy.astro';

const facts = [
  { k: 'City', v: 'Berlin-Kreuzberg' },
  { k: 'Year', v: '2013' },
  { k: 'Height', v: '6 stories' },
  { k: 'Homes', v: '19' },
  { k: 'Architects', v: 'ifau + Jesko Fezer · Heide & von Beckerath' },
  { k: 'Tenure', v: 'Baugruppe' },
  { k: 'Signature', v: 'Continuous wraparound balcony' },
];

const gallery = [
  { placeholder: 'Wraparound balcony — photo needed', cap: 'The continuous balcony: one porch shared by every floor' },
  { placeholder: 'Garden — photo needed', cap: 'The garden the house holds at the center of the block' },
  { placeholder: 'Interior — photo needed', cap: 'Simple shell interiors, finished by residents' },
];
---

<CaseStudy
  title="R50"
  subtitle="Proof the type works at small scale."
  heroPlaceholder="R50 hero — wraparound balcony facade (photo needed)"
  facts={facts}
  gallery={gallery}
  prev={{ href: '/spreefeld/', label: 'Spreefeld, Berlin' }}
  next={{ href: '/sargfabrik/', label: 'Sargfabrik' }}
>
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-[760px] px-6 pt-16 sm:px-12">
    <p class="text-[21px] leading-[1.62]">
      Nineteen households pooled as a Baugruppe, hired two small offices, and built a six-story
      house in the middle of an ordinary Kreuzberg block. The move everyone remembers is the
      balcony: a continuous open walkway wrapping every floor, so the whole building shares one long
      porch. Below, a garden; inside, a workshop, laundry, and a roof terrace; the apartments
      themselves were delivered simply and finished by their owners.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-6 text-[21px] leading-[1.62]">
      What to steal: the scale. R50 is the proof that the courtyard-block instincts — shared
      circulation you actually meet people on, common rooms instead of bigger flats — work on a
      single infill lot with nineteen homes, no master plan required.
    </p>
  </section>

  <Fragment slot="lessons">
    <li>
      One lot, one group, nineteen homes — the entry ticket is smaller than the atlas's famous
      projects suggest.
    </li>
    <li>
      A continuous balcony is gallery access at its cheapest: circulation and social life in one
      element.
    </li>
    <li>Delivering apartments as simple shells put the budget into shared parts.</li>
    <li>An architect-led Baugruppe closed in Berlin in 2013; the model is the lesson, not the address.</li>
  </Fragment>

  <Fragment slot="sources">
    Sources: architects' project pages —
    <a href="https://heidevonbeckerath.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">heidevonbeckerath.com</a>
  </Fragment>
</CaseStudy>
